Two unchecked-NULL deaths during ARM64EC child boot on iOS, both of which
destroyed the diagnostic that would have named the real bug.

AllocatorHooks: threads that enter FEX without passing through InitCRTThread
(wine loader threads during EC child boot) had no rpmalloc thread heap, so
every hook returned NULL. Lazily initialize at the hook boundary —
rpmalloc_is_thread_initialized() is a cheap TLS read.

LogManager MFmtImpl: the allocator returning NULL on the logging thread fed an
unchecked memmove(NULL) inside vformat, killing the process while formatting a
message. Probe the allocator and drop the message instead of crashing; the
child surviving matters more than the text. A raw write(2) emit was tried first
and ALSO crashed — in a pseudo-process child the FEX pool copy's .data is
cloned, so the CRT file table and rpmalloc state are invalid there and nothing
in this DLL that touches its own globals is safe. Message identity is parked in
LastDroppedFmt, a plain pointer store that cannot fail.

Later work proved the NULLs were downstream of Wine returning
STATUS_NO_MEMORY, not of FEX itself — but hardening the failure path is what
made that diagnosable at all.

